      "writtenOn": "2026-09-01T19:50:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I have not tested performance, but `OpImageFetch` requires an LOD parameters whereas `OpImageRead` does not. It\u0027s unclear whether one is faster than the other. Since `OpImageRead` does not care about LOD, it can omit that logic. But `OpImageFetch` is backed by the texture cache whereas a storage texture backed by the L2 cache. (Texture cache is almost always faster because it\u0027s read only whereas L2 cache must handle writes).\n\n`OpImageFetch` does not need to know the format. If you look at \n\"TextureReadTexture.asm.frag\" and `TextureReadSampler.asm.frag` both the `OpImageFetch` ops are decorated with \"Unknown.\" Instead, the pixel format gets filled in by the driver at runtime.\n\n`OpImageFetch` explicitly operates on non-storage textures/does not require storage usage.\n\nPrior to this change, we only supported `OpImageFetch` on `sampler2D`. If we wanted to read from a texture, we always bumped the texture to storage, which was unnecessary. Performance concerns aside, storage textures are less supported. Prior to this change, Dawn did not support readonly storage textures:\nhttps://developer.chrome.com/blog/new-in-webgpu-124\n\nSo we really just want a non-storage readonly texture2D.\n\nWe still maintain both readonly storage and readonly texture2D. \nIf you want texture2D, you omit the pixel format declaration:\n`layout(binding\u003d0) readonly texture2D t;` \n\nIf you want storage texture2D you include the pixel format declaration:\n`layout(binding\u003d0, rgba8) readonly texture2D t;`",
      "parentUuid": "1c91036c_01193368",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 13,
        "startChar": 2,
        "endLine": 13,
        "endChar": 147
      },
      "revId": "b86a0f33638730d1069acdbbe84025cf9b3e39c8",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
